Environment: Linux (hoare.cs.umsl.edu), gcc, vim

===========================================================
How to compile the project:
===========================================================
    make

===========================================================
Example of how to run the project:
===========================================================
    ./oss -n 3 -s 2 -t 4 -i 0.6 -f log.txt
    ./oss -n 18 -s 5 -t 2.5 -i 0.2 -f oss.log
    ./oss -h

Command-line options:
  -h            Print help and exit
  -n proc       Total number of processes to launch (default: 18, max: 18)
  -s simul      Maximum simultaneous processes (default: 18, capped at 18)
  -t timelimit  Upper bound on a process's total CPU burst in seconds (default: 2.5)
  -i fraction   Minimum fraction of a second between child launches (default: 0.5)
  -f logfile    Log file name (default: oss.log)

===========================================================
Project Overview
===========================================================
This project simulates a Round-Robin process scheduler (OSS).

Files:
  oss.c     - Main OS simulator. Forks worker processes, maintains the
              process control table (PCB array in shared memory), runs the
              Round-Robin scheduler using a ready queue and a blocked queue,
              and writes a detailed log. Also prints process table to both
              screen and log every 500 ms of simulated time.
  worker.c  - Simulated user process. Waits on the message queue for a time
              quantum, then replies indicating: full quantum used, partial
              quantum + I/O block (20% chance), or termination.
  shared.h  - Shared data structures: SimClock, PCB, SharedData, Message,
              and all #define constants (quantum size, block time, IPC keys).
  queue.h   - Inline circular FIFO queue for ready and blocked queues.
  Makefile  - Builds both executables with .c.o suffix rules.
  README    - This file.

IPC Design:
  Shared Memory: One SharedData segment holds the simulated clock and the
    process control table. Key derived with ftok("/tmp", 'A').
  Message Queue: Single queue for bidirectional communication.
    OSS sends to worker using mtype = worker's real PID.
    Worker replies to OSS using mtype = OSS's PID (getppid()).
    Key derived with ftok("/tmp", 'B').
  ftok() is used for all keys so the project is safe on a shared server
    (hoare) and will not collide with other students' IPC resources.

Scheduling Algorithm — Round Robin:
  - New processes are added to the back of the ready queue.
  - OSS always dispatches the process at the front of the ready queue.
  - Full quantum used → process goes to the back of the ready queue.
  - Partial quantum used (20% I/O chance) → process goes to blocked queue
    and will be unblocked after 100 ms of simulated time.
  - Process terminates → PCB is cleared, process is reaped with waitpid().
  - The ready queue is printed before every scheduling decision.
  - The process table (including blocked list) is printed every 500 ms of
    simulated time to both the log file and stdout.

CPU Utilization (per spec):
  Calculated as the total clock time incremented by OSS for reasons OTHER
  than a worker running (i.e. scheduling overhead + idle time) divided by
  total simulated clock time. This matches the spec wording: "calculated by
  storing up the total time you incremented the clock that was not from
  getting a message back from a process."

Termination:
  - Normal: all -n processes have completed.
  - Signal: SIGINT and SIGTERM call the signal handler which prints the
    final report, kills all children, removes shared memory and message
    queue, and exits.
  - Timeout: SIGALRM fires after 3 real seconds (alarm(3)). The signal
    handler prints "OSS: caught signal 14 — cleaning up and terminating."
    to both stderr and the log, then does a full cleanup.
  - The log also checks the 3-second wall-clock each iteration and prints
    "OSS: 3-second real-time limit reached — terminating." before breaking
    out of the loop if the alarm has not fired yet.
  - Log output is capped at 10,000 lines to prevent runaway files.

Cleanup:
  All IPC is removed on exit. After any run, verify with:
    ipcs -m | grep $(id -u)   # should be empty
    ipcs -q | grep $(id -u)   # should be empty

